=========================================================
OOAD RELATIONSHIPS – INTERVIEW NOTES (COPYABLE)
=========================================================

Relationships define how classes or objects are connected in an object-oriented system.

The six most common OOAD relationships are:

1. Association
2. Aggregation
3. Composition
4. Dependency
5. Inheritance
6. Realization

=========================================================
1. ASSOCIATION
=========================================================

Definition
----------
Association represents a relationship where one object knows or uses another object.

It is the most general relationship.

Represents
----------
USES / KNOWS

Ownership
---------
No ownership.

Object Lifetime
---------------
Independent.

Notation (UML)
--------------
Solid line

A -------- B

Example
-------

Teacher -------- Student

A teacher teaches students.

Students can exist without the teacher.

Teachers can exist without students.

More Examples
-------------

• Customer ↔ Order
• Doctor ↔ Patient
• Employee ↔ Department
• Driver ↔ Car

C++ Example
-----------

class Student {};

class Teacher
{
public:
    void teach(Student& s)
    {
    }
};

Interview Answer
----------------

"Association is a relationship where two independent objects are connected. Neither object owns the other."



=========================================================
2. AGGREGATION
=========================================================

Definition
----------

Aggregation is a special type of Association that represents a HAS-A relationship with weak ownership.

Represents
----------

HAS-A

Ownership
----------

Weak Ownership

Object Lifetime
---------------

Independent.

Child can exist without Parent.

Notation (UML)
--------------

Hollow Diamond

Parent ◇──── Child

Example
-------

Department HAS-A Employees

If Department is deleted,

Employees still exist.

Other Examples
--------------

• Team HAS-A Players
• University HAS-A Professors
• Library HAS-A Books
• School HAS-A Teachers

C++ Example
-----------

class Employee
{
};

class Department
{
private:
    vector<Employee*> employees;
};

Department uses Employee objects.

Employees are created elsewhere.

Interview Answer
----------------

"Aggregation is a weak HAS-A relationship. The parent references child objects but does not own their lifetime."



=========================================================
3. COMPOSITION
=========================================================

Definition
----------

Composition is a strong HAS-A relationship.

Parent owns the child.

Represents
----------

HAS-A

Ownership
----------

Strong Ownership

Object Lifetime
---------------

Dependent.

If Parent is destroyed,

Child is automatically destroyed.

Notation (UML)
--------------

Filled Diamond

Parent ◆──── Child

Example
-------

House HAS-A Room

Destroy House

↓

Rooms are destroyed.

More Examples
-------------

• Car HAS-A Engine
• Computer HAS-A CPU
• Human HAS-A Heart
• Book HAS-A Pages

C++ Example
-----------

class Engine
{
};

class Car
{
private:
    Engine engine;
};

Engine cannot exist independently of Car.

Interview Answer
----------------

"Composition is a strong HAS-A relationship where the parent owns the child object. The child's lifetime depends on the parent's lifetime."



=========================================================
4. DEPENDENCY
=========================================================

Definition
----------

Dependency represents a temporary USES relationship.

One object depends on another only for a short duration.

Represents
----------

USES

Ownership
----------

No ownership.

Object Lifetime
---------------

Independent.

Notation (UML)
--------------

Dashed Arrow

A ---------> B

Example
-------

Printer uses Document.

Printer doesn't store Document.

Document exists independently.

More Examples
-------------

• Logger used by Service
• PaymentService uses BankAPI
• ReportGenerator uses Printer
• Controller uses Validator

C++ Example
-----------

class Document
{
};

class Printer
{
public:

    void print(Document& doc)
    {
    }
};

Document is passed only during function execution.

Interview Answer
----------------

"Dependency is a temporary relationship where one class uses another class without owning or storing it."



=========================================================
5. INHERITANCE
=========================================================

Definition
----------

Inheritance allows one class to acquire properties and behavior of another.

Represents
----------

IS-A

Ownership
----------

Not applicable.

Object Lifetime
---------------

Independent.

Notation (UML)
--------------

Solid Line + Hollow Triangle

Dog --------|> Animal

Example
-------

Dog IS-A Animal

Car IS-A Vehicle

Manager IS-A Employee

C++ Example
-----------

class Animal
{
};

class Dog : public Animal
{
};

Interview Answer
----------------

"Inheritance represents an IS-A relationship where a derived class inherits the properties and behavior of a base class."



=========================================================
6. REALIZATION
=========================================================

Definition
----------

Realization means a class implements an interface (or abstract class with pure virtual functions).

Represents
----------

IMPLEMENTS

Ownership
----------

None.

Notation (UML)
--------------

Dashed Line + Hollow Triangle

Circle ----- - -|> Shape

Example
-------

Shape (Interface)

↓

Circle implements Shape

C++ Example
-----------

class Shape
{
public:
    virtual void draw() = 0;
};

class Circle : public Shape
{
public:

    void draw() override
    {
    }
};

Interview Answer
----------------

"Realization represents an IMPLEMENTS relationship where a class provides the implementation for an interface or abstract class."



=========================================================
ASSOCIATION vs AGGREGATION vs COMPOSITION
=========================================================

Association
-----------
Meaning:
Knows / Uses

Ownership:
None

Lifetime:
Independent

Example:
Teacher ↔ Student

---------------------------------------------------------

Aggregation
-----------
Meaning:
Weak HAS-A

Ownership:
Weak

Lifetime:
Independent

Example:
Department HAS-A Employees

---------------------------------------------------------

Composition
-----------
Meaning:
Strong HAS-A

Ownership:
Strong

Lifetime:
Dependent

Example:
Car HAS-A Engine

Golden Rule

Association
↓

Aggregation
↓

Composition

Strength increases.



=========================================================
INHERITANCE vs REALIZATION
=========================================================

Inheritance
-----------
Relationship:
IS-A

Purpose:
Reuse implementation and behavior

Example:
Dog IS-A Animal

---------------------------------------------------------

Realization
-----------
Relationship:
IMPLEMENTS

Purpose:
Provide implementation for an interface

Example:
Circle implements Shape



